WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:01.493
Part six.

00:00:01.493 --> 00:00:03.772
Screenshots and image tools.

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:09.285
Here's the third row's confirmation, and there's no free shipping anywhere.

00:00:09.285 --> 00:00:13.403
When you report a bug, a clear picture beats a long description.

00:00:16.633 --> 00:00:19.230
The Capture tab has six kinds of capture.

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:24.322
Blue buttons save straight away; the dashed ones open the shot in the editor first.

00:00:24.333 --> 00:00:30.075
Capture the visible area, with a three-second countdown in case you need to open a menu first.

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:33.529
The editor opens in a window of its own.

00:00:33.529 --> 00:00:37.788
Box the problem, add an arrow, and blur anything private.

00:00:42.267 --> 00:00:43.063
Save it.

00:00:43.063 --> 00:00:47.559
The file name carries a prefix and the time, so it's easy to find later.

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:53.213
Every capture is copied to the clipboard as well, ready to paste into a bug report.

00:00:53.213 --> 00:00:57.086
And the Capture tab keeps the latest ones under Recent captures.

00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:58.378
Need just one element?

00:00:58.378 --> 00:01:00.745
Click Pick, then choose it on the page.

00:01:05.033 --> 00:01:12.213
And Image Diff compares two screenshots and paints the pixels that differ in magenta, which is handy for visual checks.

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:21.999
Between the two versions, a price and a button have changed, and Image Diff shows exactly where.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:29.123
There are four ways to look at it: the changes in pink, a line you drag between the two, a blink, or side by side.

00:01:30.267 --> 00:01:34.980
Click Save result to download the comparison, and open it to see it up close.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:42.143
The pixels that differ are painted magenta: the price of Mountain Roads, and the Add to cart button that now says Sold out.

00:01:42.167 --> 00:01:45.220
Next, we'll turn the scenario into real test code.
